nds":"2026-08-16T20:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Wet Mountains between 6300 and 10000Ft; Wet Mountains above 10000 Ft; Pueblo Vicinity/Pueblo County Below 6300 Feet","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.756c47587b9063b4ff8ae253c038ec09a9c93d18.002.2","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.756c47587b9063b4ff8ae253c038ec09a9c93d18.002.2"},{"event":"Flash Flood Watch","headline":"Flash Flood Watch issued August 15 at 4:59PM MDT until August 15 at 8:00PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flash flooding and debris flows caused by excessive rainfall\ncontinue to be possible over the Aspen Acres burn scar.\n\n* WHERE...Aspen Acres Burn Scar\n\n* WHEN...Until 8 PM MDT this evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Heavy rainfall over the Aspen Acres burn scar is expected\nup to and during the period of the watch. Residents near the Aspen\nAcres burn scar should prepare for potential debris flow. Be sure\nto stay up to date with information from local authorities. Heavy\nrainfall could trigger flash flooding of low-lying areas, street\nflooding, and debris flows in and near recent wildfire burn scars.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- National Weather Service Meteorologists are forecasting\nthunderstorms with heavy rainfall over the burn scar, which may\nlead to flash flooding and debris flows.\n- Some locations that may experience flash flooding include...\nBeulah, San Isabel, and highways 165 and 78 in and around the\nAspen Acres Burn Scar.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-08-15T20:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Wet Mountains between 6300 and 10000Ft; Wet Mountains above 10000 Ft; Pueblo Vicinity/Pueblo County Below 6300 Feet","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.756c47587b9063b4ff8ae253c038ec09a9c93d18.002.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.756c47587b9063b4ff8ae253c038ec09a9c93d18.002.1"},{"event":"Special Weather Statement","headline":"Special Weather Statement issued August 15 at 4:47PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"At 446 PM MDT, Doppler radar was tracking a cluster of strong\nthunderstorms 11 miles north of Greenhorn Mountain, or 26 miles\nsouthwest of Pueblo, moving east at 10 mph.\n\nHAZARD...Wind gusts up to 40 mph and half inch hail.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Gusty winds could knock down tree limbs and blow around\nunsecured objects. Minor hail damage to vegetation is\npossible.\n\nLocations impacted include...\nBeulah.","ends":"2026-08-15T17:15:00-06:00","severity":"Moderate","areaDesc":"Wet Mountains between 6300 and 10000Ft; Wet Mountains above 10000 Ft; Pueblo Vicinity/Pueblo County Below 6300 Feet","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.72a8030424e13dfc656ae38b34575f7ff60f6df6.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.72a8030424e13dfc656ae38b34575f7ff60f6df6.001.1"},{"event":"Flash Flood Warning","headline":"Flash Flood Warning issued August 15 at 4:31PM MDT until August 15 at 7:30PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"FFWPUB\n\nThe National Weather Service in Pueblo has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nThe Middle Aspen Acres Burn Scar in...\nEast Central Custer County in southeastern Colorado...\nWest Central Pueblo County in southeastern Colorado...\n\n* Until 730 PM MDT.\n\n* At 431 PM MDT, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain over the Middle Aspen Acres Burn Scar. Between 0.2 and\n0.5 inches of rain have fallen. The expected rainfall rate is 0.5\nto 1 inch in 1 hour. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected to begin\nshortly. Excessive rainfall over the burn scar will result in\ndebris flow moving through the North Saint Charles, South Creek,\nSquirrel Creek, Middle Creek, and North Creek Drainages. The debris\nflow can consist of rock, mud, vegetation and other loose\nmaterials.\n\nHAZARD...Life threatening flash flooding. Thunderstorms producing\nflash flooding in and around the Middle Aspen Acres Burn\nScar.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Life threatening flash flooding of areas in and around the\nMiddle Aspen Acres Burn Scar.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nHighway 165, Beulah, Highway 78, stream crossings, and county roads\nwithin and immediately downstream of the burn scar.\n\nThis includes the following high risk locations... Northcreek Road\ncounty line to Red Mountain Camp and Northcreek Road from Red\nMountain Camp to Beulah.","ends":"2026-08-15T19:30:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Custer, CO; Pueblo, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d21a03753a4730493fbd369f7f512faab0256f7a.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d21a03753a4730493fbd369f7f512faab0256f7a.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"PUB","issued":"2026-08-15T19:12:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KPUB 151912 AFDPUB Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Pueblo CO 112 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Thunderstorms capable of producing heavy rainfall and flash flooding continue through Sunday with a few strong to severe thunderstorms across the southeast plains both days. - Thunderstorm activity a little less widespread for Monday as drier air works in from the","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=PUB&issuedby=PUB&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":{"office":"PUB","issued":"2026-08-15T17:34:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FLUS45 KPUB 151734 HWOPUB Hazardous Weather Outlook National Weather Service Pueblo CO 1134 AM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026 COZ058>089-093>099-161745- Western Mosquito Range/East Lake County Above 11000 Feet- Leadville Vicinity/Lake County Below 11000 Feet- Eastern Sawatch Mountains Above 11000 Feet- Western Chaffee County Between 9000 and 11000 Feet- Central Chaffee County Below 9000 Feet- Western Mos","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=PUB&issuedby=PUB&product=HWO&format=CI"},"fwf":{"office":"PUB","issued":"2026-08-15T22:54:00+00:00","snippet":".Discussion... Afternoon and evening thunderstorms capable of heavy rainfall and flash flooding will con